Pace & Pulse — change log
=========================
Newest first. One entry per published version (the number shown at the bottom of every page).
Each entry starts with "In short:" (one line, shown in the app's update message), then what changed and why.
Algorithm changes name the old and new algorithm ids.

v32 — 2026-10-07
  In short: New Reports tab with day-by-day heart rate, sleep, stress, body battery, steps and weight pages.
  Reports (replaces Records in the tab bar; Records is reached from Reports): a Garmin-style list grouped as Health stats, Training and Activities.
  Heart rate, Sleep, Stress, Body battery and Steps open a report page with Today (full-day chart; ‹ › or the date picker move between days),
    7 days, 4 weeks and Year. Weight shows the weight log, your goal line and progress.
  Overview: Heart rate, Sleep, Stress and Body battery are now charts of your latest day; new Steps (ring) and Weight (progress bar) cards.
    Older saved layouts get these cards once, at the end (Customize to move or remove them).
  The Garmin sync now stores intraday samples as {t, v} maps (Firestore rejects nested arrays); the app reads both shapes.
  No algorithm changes.

v31 — 2026-10-07
  In short: New all-day pages for resting heart rate, stress and body battery from your Garmin watch.
  The Garmin export job writes one document per day to users/{uid}/daily/{YYYY-MM-DD}; the app now reads the last
    400 days (online accounts only) and shows them on three trend pages (7 days / 4 weeks / Year) and three optional
    overview cards (Customize → Add cards): Resting heart rate, Stress, Body battery.
  Field names are read tolerantly (restingHR, stressAvg, bodyBatteryHigh/Low, or a series for each), so a renamed field doesn't break the app.
  Values are shown as Garmin supplies them; no new algorithm, so no algorithm ids change.

v29 — 2026-10-06
  In short: Running goals now count only real runs at least as long as the goal.
  A goal like 10K at 7:00/km is reached only by a run of 10 km or longer (1% GPS slack) with an average pace
    of 7:00/km or faster. Progress uses your best such run in the last 12 weeks; the bar starts at your typical
    (median) pace on those runs in the 12 weeks before you set the goal. If there's no long-enough run, it says so.
  Race predictions and fastest 10 km stretches inside runs no longer count.
  Why: the goal showed "on target" from the VO₂max race prediction without a run that actually did it.

v28 — 2026-10-06
  In short: Set running and weight goals in Profile — see your progress and get coaching toward them.
  Profile → Goals: a running goal (5K / 10K / half / marathon or any distance, target pace like 6:00/km, optional
    race date) and a weight goal (target and starting weight, optional date). Progress bars show how far you've
    come (running counts from your level 12 weeks before the goal was set): running uses your current level (the faster of the race prediction and your best
    effort at that distance in the last 12 weeks); weight uses your current weight. Also shows your PR.
  New "Goals" card on the overview (added once to saved layouts too); "Edit →" jumps to the Profile card.
  The AI coach now gets your goals, progress, time left and recent weight log, and aims its advice at them.
  Weight changes are now logged (settings.wlog) so the trend is known.
  RACEalg2 gains RACE.at() for any distance (penalty interpolated on log distance); the 5K/10K/half/marathon
    predictions are unchanged, so the id stays RACEalg2.
  Why: requested goal parameters the AI can use, with progress bars for running and weight.

v27 — 2026-10-06
  In short: Google sign-in now works from the home-screen app.
  In the app saved to the iPhone home screen, "Continue with Google" opened Google in a separate window that
    could not hand the result back, so nothing happened. The home-screen app now goes to Google in the same
    window and Google returns straight to the app, which signs you in (Firebase Authentication with the Google
    token). "Save account online" with Google finishes the upload of your workouts and settings on return.
    A cancelled sign-in says so. Browsers keep the Google popup.
  Needs once: the app address https://f2cfpkjp8z-alt.github.io/run/ as an Authorized redirect URI of the
    project's Google OAuth web client.
  Why: Google sign-in did nothing in the installed app.

v26 — 2026-10-06
  In short: Sign in with your Pace & Pulse account — everything is saved online, and pages open faster.
  The app now has its own Firebase project built in: sign up / sign in uses Firebase Authentication
    (email + password with "Forgot password?", or Google). New visitors start at sign-in; the option to paste
    your own Firebase config is gone. On-device profiles still work for browsers that have them, with
    "Save account online" to move them up.
  Saved in the private online account (users/{uid}): profile, settings, overview layout, appearance
    (theme, font, text size), the Gemini API key and model, and the saved AI opinions. A key that was
    saved in this browser moves into the account on first online sign-in.
  Computed results are saved with each workout (field an): imported runs are analysed once and the results
    are reused on every page load. They are recalculated only when an algorithm version changes or when
    max HR, resting HR or sex changes. No algorithm changed — the numbers are identical (all ids unchanged).
  AI coach default model is now Gemini 3.5 Flash-Lite (gemini-3.5-flash-lite); saved 2.5 Flash moves to it.
  Why: one shared online account for all users, with nothing lost when the browser is cleared, and less
    work on every page load.

v25 — 2026-10-06
  In short: New Asphalt theme — dark grey with orange, and every chart in its own colour.
  Asphalt (Profile → Appearance) is a flat neutral-grey dark theme with an orange accent, without the tinted glow.
    In it each measurement has its own colour on every chart: heart rate red, pace blue, elevation grey,
    VO₂max teal, endurance purple, fitness blue / fatigue pink / form yellow, distance orange, load amber,
    efficiency green, race predictions magenta, heart-rate drift yellow.
  Charts now ask for a colour per measurement (--pace, --vo2, …) instead of the generic series colour; the
    other themes map these to their existing colours, so they look the same as before.
  Why: requested a Strava-like dark mode where charts aren't all one colour.

v24 — 2026-10-06
  In short: The account menu now opens over the page instead of shifting the layout.
  Tapping the profile picture used to push the menu into the header, widening the page and leaving an empty strip on
    the right. The menu is now a floating panel anchored under the picture (a later ".card" rule was overriding its
    position), with a small open animation and scrolling if the screen is short.
  Why: it should behave like a native app menu.

v23 — 2026-10-06
  In short: Clear import progress and a list of the runs you just imported.
  While importing, a progress bar shows "Reading 3 of 7 · 2 runs" and "Saving…". When done, a results sheet
    shows short coloured counts (+5 new runs, 2 already imported, 1 not running, 1 file unreadable, Max HR ↑)
    and the imported runs with route, date, distance, time and pace — tap one to open it, or "All workouts".
  Why: the old single line of plain text was long and hard to read on a phone.

v22 — 2026-10-06
  In short: The Feed tab only appears once your account is saved online.
  The Feed tab is hidden from the top bar and the bottom bar until you save your account online; opening
    #feed without an online account goes to the overview. The bottom bar spreads the remaining 5 tabs evenly.
  Why: the feed can't be used on an on-device profile, so the tab was a dead end.

v21 — 2026-10-06
  In short: More Garmin-style views: load focus, fitness age card, zone colours, VO₂max bands.
  New LFalg1 Load focus card (Garmin's Training Load Focus): your 4-week load split into low aerobic,
    high aerobic and anaerobic (Seiler 3-zone split: under 80% / 80–90% / over 90% of max HR), each against a target range, with "Balanced" or "… shortage". Also on the
    Intensity trend page.
  New Fitness age card: fitness age vs your real age ("5 years younger"), with VO₂max, resting HR, BMI and
    vigorous minutes per week, each with its status badge.
  Heart-rate zones use Garmin's colours everywhere: Z1 grey, Z2 blue, Z3 green, Z4 orange, Z5 red.
  VO₂max charts show the Poor…Superior bands for your age behind the line, like Garmin.
  Race predictions show ▲ faster / ▼ slower than 4 weeks ago.
  Fitness trend page: a training status history strip (one coloured cell per day).
  Per-run load is now also kept per heart-rate zone (needed for load focus); all other numbers unchanged.
  Why: to make every view read like Garmin Connect.

v20 — 2026-10-06
  In short: Garmin-style training status, and one status per gauge.
  New TSalg1 (training status), like Garmin: Productive, Maintaining, Recovery, Peaking, Unproductive,
    Detraining or Overreaching, from your 3-week VO₂max trend and your load ratio (ACWRalg2). The card shows the
    status in its colour, the VO₂max trend arrow and a Garmin-style acute-load bar (low / optimal / high /
    very high with a marker). Fitness, fatigue and form moved behind the ? on phones. Replaces the form-only
    label (Fresh / Balanced / Building / Overreaching).
  Gauges no longer print their own label under the number; the coloured status badge sits inside the arc
    instead (VO₂max, endurance, training load), so each status shows once.
  Why: duplicated statuses, and training status should read like Garmin's.

v19 — 2026-10-06
  In short: Cleaner phone screens with ? buttons, and colour-coded status badges.
  Phones: captions, hints, fine print and long explanations are hidden; each card that has them gets a ?
    button that shows them (and "How it's calculated" when available). Desktop keeps everything visible.
  One status language everywhere: a coloured badge with a word — Excellent (purple/blue), Good (green),
    Normal (grey), Caution (orange), Bad (red). Used for the VO₂max rating, endurance level, training status,
    training load, intensity balance (80/20), each endurance factor (Strong / Fair / Low / Very low), heart-rate
    drift on a workout (Solid / Normal / High / Very high) and the status figures on the trend pages.
  Why: too much text on phones, and statuses should be readable at a glance.

v18 — 2026-10-06
  In short: The version line stays at the bottom of the screen on every page.
  The page now always fills the screen height, so the version / "Check for updates" line no longer jumps up
    on pages with little content (e.g. a 7-day trend page or an empty feed).
  Why: the footer moved up and down depending on page length.

v17 — 2026-10-06
  In short: Update messages say what's new; chart tooltips are dark.
  The update banner and the "Updating…" message now include a one-line summary of what changed, read from this
    change.log ("In short:" lines; several versions are listed if you skipped some). After an automatic update
    a "What's new" banner shows it once.
  Chart and map tooltips and the update toast use a dark background in every theme (they were white on dark
    themes).
  Why: to know what an update brings; white pop-ups were glaring.

v16 — 2026-10-06
  In short: Fitness age now counts resting heart rate and BMI like Garmin.
  RATEalg2 → RATEalg3 (fitness age). Why: it compared VO₂max with Cooper medians, which barely change from
    20 to 39, so 46.8 at age 37 showed fitness age 20 while Garmin shows 32. Now, like Garmin, it also counts
    resting heart rate (+0.155 ml/kg/min per bpm below average, NTNU fitness model) and BMI (+0.75 per point
    under 25, Jackson), then compares with the average healthy adult of each age (HUNT Fitness Study,
    n = 3,816). Your numbers (37, VO₂max 46.8–48, RHR 46, BMI 24.2) → 30–34; Garmin 32. Garmin also counts
    vigorous minutes, which the app can't see. The Poor…Superior rating is unchanged (Cooper, as Garmin).
    Set resting HR, height and weight in Profile.

v15 — 2026-10-06
  In short: The app updates itself; pull down to check for updates.
  The app updates itself — useful when it runs from the iPhone home screen, where there is no reload button.
    It checks for a new version when opened, when you come back to it, every 15 minutes while open, and when
    you pull down at the top of any page (pull-to-refresh, which also redraws your data). When a new version
    is found it shows "Updating to version N…" and restarts on its own; if you are busy (a dialog is open,
    an import is running, you are customizing the overview or typing) it waits with an "Update now" banner.
    The version line at the bottom also has "Check for updates".
  Why: home-screen apps on iOS can't be reloaded, so new versions only arrived after closing the app.

v14 — 2026-10-06
  In short: Feels like a native app: no page zoom, pinch-zoom on maps, press feedback.
  Feels like a native app on phones:
    - no page zoom: pinch and double-tap zoom are blocked (iOS ignores the viewport setting, so its pinch
      gesture is blocked in code); text boxes are 16 px+ so iOS no longer zooms in when you tap one.
      Bigger text is in Profile → Appearance → Text size.
    - the route map gets its own two-finger pinch zoom, like a maps app.
    - no grey tap flash, no long-press link/image menus, no accidental text selection (still selectable in
      text boxes, formulas and the AI coach), no rubber-band bounce or pull-to-refresh.
    - buttons, cards and tabs shrink slightly when pressed; desktop-only hover effects are off on touch screens.
    - the top bar extends under the iPhone status bar / notch when installed to the home screen.
  Why: so the app behaves like an installed app, not a web page.

v13 — 2026-10-06
  In short: Add to Home Screen / Install app with the Pace & Pulse icon.
  Installable web app. "Add to Home Screen" on iPhone/iPad and "Install app" in Chrome/Android/desktop now
    use the Pace & Pulse icon and name and open full-screen without browser bars: web app manifest, icons
    (180 px Apple touch icon, 192/512 px, maskable 512 px for Android's shapes, SVG and 32 px favicons),
    iOS meta tags, and a service worker that serves the last loaded version when offline (network first,
    so updates still arrive; map tiles, Firebase and Gemini are never cached).
  Why: to save the app to the home screen like a normal app.

v12 — 2026-10-06
  In short: Shows a banner when a newer version is published.
  Update check: a few seconds after opening (and whenever you return to the tab) the app asks GitHub Pages
    for the latest version, bypassing the browser cache. If it is newer, a banner offers "Update now".
    Why: GitHub Pages lets browsers keep the page for 10 minutes, so new versions didn't show right away.

v11 — 2026-10-06
  Import button icon changed to a file with a plus; the old up-arrow looked like the Share icon.
  Workouts can be shared as an image (workout page → 🖼 Share image), no account needed: OpenStreetMap
    map, route only or no map; 4:5 post or 9:16 story; distance, time, pace, avg HR, elevation gain and
    loss, cadence, VO₂max estimate, laps (pace bars, fastest highlighted), name/date and app name — each can
    be switched off. Share (phone share sheet) or download as PNG. Choices are remembered on the device.
  Elevation loss (descent) is now computed for every run, alongside the existing gain.
  The online "Share" button on a workout is renamed "Share link" to tell it apart.
  Why: to post runs with a clean, customizable card.

v10 — 2026-10-05
  Auto max heart rate now says where it comes from and when it changes. Profile shows the value in use
    ("200 bpm, auto — 220 − age" or "199 bpm, auto — detected in <run>, <date>"), the VO₂max card shows it,
    the run that set it is marked ★ "sets your max", and an import that raises it says so
    ("New max heart rate detected: 199 bpm (was 197) …"). Auto = the higher of 220 − age and the highest
    6-second peak in your runs.
  HRMAXalg3 → HRMAXalg4. Why: the glitch check compared runs with each other, so with only a few runs a real
    hard effort (your Benchmark Run, 191 bpm) was thrown away as a "spike". Now each run is checked on its
    own: a 6-s peak is a spike only if it sits more than 10 bpm above that run's 30-s peak.
  Explanation added: height and weight are not used — they don't predict max heart rate; age does.
  Why: so you can see which max HR every number is based on and trust that it follows your runs.

v9 — 2026-10-05
  Every overview card opens its own trend page (#m-…) with 7 days / 4 weeks / Year buttons, always starting
    on 7 days: VO₂max, endurance, fitness/fatigue/form, race predictions, distance, training load,
    intensity mix and efficiency, each with period stats, charts and the workouts behind them.
  HRMAXalg2 → HRMAXalg3. Why: VO₂max still read 46 vs Garmin's 48 on the same files. Max HR is the biggest
    lever (≈1 VO₂max point per 3 bpm). Peaks now use 6-second averages (30-second ones sat 2–4 bpm under
    the real peak) and the age default is Garmin's 220 − age instead of Tanaka. With your age or Garmin's
    max HR in Profile, VO₂max matches Garmin's; the files alone peak at 197 bpm, which gives 46.7.
  ENDalg1 → ENDalg2. Why: the training factors multiplied the whole score, so VO₂max 48 with a few weeks of
    history scored ~2,900 ("Recreational"). Now 100 × VO₂max is the base and training adds 0.85–1.6× on top
    (same volume / long-run / durability factors); your files now score ~5,200 (Intermediate); the long-run and
    weekly-hours factors are what move it.
  ZONEalg1 → ZONEalg2. Why: Garmin's default zones are % of max HR (60/70/80/90); time in zones now
    matches Garmin Connect. Resting HR is still used for training load (TRIMP needs heart-rate reserve).
  ACWRalg1 → ACWRalg2. Why: plain 7/28-day sums jump when a hard day leaves the window; exponentially
    weighted averages (Williams 2017) are smoother and the better injury-risk predictor.
  Reviewed and kept: GAPalg1 (Minetti), LOADalg2 (Banister TRIMP), FFalg1 (42/7-day Banister model),
    DRIFTalg2 (Friel Pa:HR), BESTalg2, RACEalg2, EFalg1, RATEalg2 (Cooper norms).

v8 — 2026-10-05
  VO2alg2 → VO2alg3. Why: on 7 real Garmin files that Garmin rates ~48, VO2alg2 gave ~32. VO2alg2 reported on
    Daniels' VDOT (race-performance) scale, which sits far below lab and Garmin values for most recreational
    runners. VO2alg3 uses the Garmin/Firstbeat approach: ACSM oxygen cost of grade-adjusted pace ÷ share of
    VO₂max from %HRmax (Swain: %HRmax = 0.64·%VO₂max + 37), weighted median of steady windows per run, and the weighted 70th percentile across recent runs (errors from heat, fatigue and drift only push estimates low, so a hot or tired day no longer drags the daily value down).
    Same files now give 44–48 with max HR 197–200. Resting HR is no longer used for VO₂max; max HR matters most.
  RACEalg1 → RACEalg2. Why: predictions must use the same scale as VO₂max, otherwise a Garmin-level VO₂max
    would predict impossible times. Race-like efforts feed VO₂max on the same scale too.
  LOADalg1 → LOADalg2: the estimate for runs without heart rate uses the new speed-at-VO₂max scale.
  Sample athlete regenerated with the VO2alg3 physiology. VO₂max, endurance score and race predictions all
    change for every profile.
  Gauges use Garmin's colours: Poor red, Fair orange, Good green, Excellent blue, Superior purple (and the
    same family for endurance tiers and load: low blue, optimal green, high orange, very high red).

v7 — 2026-10-05
  Garmin-style gauges with an arrow at your value: VO₂max (Poor → Superior for your age and sex), Endurance
    score (Recreational → Elite) and Training load (Low / Optimal / High / Very high).
  RATEalg1 → RATEalg2 (VO₂max rating): boundaries are interpolated between age decades instead of jumping at
    each decade; adds fitness age (the age whose average VO₂max equals yours).
  Height added to Profile (with BMI). VO₂max explanation now says what weight, height and age do:
    weight and height don't change a run-based VO₂max (it is already per kg); age and sex set the rating,
    fitness age and default max HR. No VO₂max calculation changed (still VO2alg2).
  Why: to show at a glance where you stand, and answer whether body size should change VO₂max.

v6 — 2026-10-05
  Import never stores the same workout twice. A workout counts as already imported when it has the same
    start minute, or starts within 2 minutes with distance within 5% (the same activity exported as .fit
    and .gpx, or a re-export). Duplicates inside one batch (e.g. a .zip plus the .fit inside it) are caught too.
    Only a copy that adds detail replaces the stored one: a full file over an activity-list CSV row, or a
    newer file version with the GPS route. The import message says how many were skipped.
    Why: re-importing used to overwrite workouts, and the same run from two file types could appear twice.

v5 — 2026-10-05
  Every measurement is now its own versioned algorithm in src/algo/ (one file each):
    VO2alg2 (VO₂max), HRMAXalg2 (max heart rate), LOADalg1 (TRIMP), ZONEalg1 (HR zones & intensity mix),
    FFalg1 (fitness/fatigue/form), ACWRalg1 (7-day load range), ENDalg1 (endurance score),
    DRIFTalg2 (HR drift), BESTalg2 (best & race-like efforts), RACEalg1 (race predictions),
    EFalg1 (aerobic efficiency), GAPalg1 (grade-adjusted pace).
    Why: so each calculation is defined in one place and can be improved as a new version (alg3, alg4…)
    with a written reason, instead of edits scattered through core.js.
  Every place a measurement appears (tiles, chart titles, workout stats, tables, Profile) is now tappable:
    it opens how the number is calculated, the formula, its inputs and limits, the current algorithm id
    and its version history. Profile → "How the numbers are made" lists all algorithms.
  Numbers are unchanged: the sample athlete's results were compared value-for-value before and after the split.
  Added this change.log. Why: keep a trace of every update and the reason for it.

v4 — 2026-10-05
  VO2alg1 → VO2alg2. Why: VO2alg1 regressed Daniels' curved oxygen cost directly on heart rate, which
    read 3–8 ml/kg/min too low on easy runs (a true 35 showed as 28–32 — the "29" seen in the chart).
    VO2alg2 regresses grade-adjusted speed on heart rate, anchored at (resting HR, 0), extends it to max HR
    to get the speed at VO₂max, then converts with Daniels' cost. Tested unbiased (±0.2) at VO₂max 35–55.
  BESTalg1 → BESTalg2. Why: tempo runs at 88% HRmax counted as "races" and pulled VO₂max down. Race-like
    efforts now need the heart rate an all-out effort of that length needs; times are scaled to the exact
    distance. Races fade out of VO₂max over 60 days instead of dropping off at day 60.
  DRIFTalg1 → DRIFTalg2. Why: drift on interval sessions is meaningless; unsteady runs are now skipped.
  HRMAXalg1 → HRMAXalg2. Why: one optical-sensor spike could set max HR; also like Garmin the age estimate
    is now a floor because most training never reaches true max.
  Charts: tick labels get enough decimals to never repeat (fixes "29 | 29 | 29"); minimum y-ranges so
    small wobbles don't look like big swings; lines clipped to the plot; shaded areas no longer bridge gaps;
    bar width capped; axis margin sized to its labels; monthly table divide-by-zero fixed.
  New look: dark "Volt" theme by default plus Ember, Glacier, Ultraviolet, Daylight and Match device; font
    style and text size settings (Profile → Appearance). Chart colours checked for colour-blind safety.
  Overview became a customizable dashboard: add, remove, resize and reorder cards (new: This week, Training
    load range, Activity calendar, Intensity mix, Personal bests, Fitness & fatigue, Endurance and
    Efficiency charts).
  AI coach with Google Gemini (key stays on the device): 4-line read on the overview and an opinion per workout.

v3 — 2026-10-05
  Version number and build time shown at the bottom of every page, stamped by build.sh.
  Why: to be sure the published page is the latest version.

v2 — 2026-10-05
  Online accounts (Firebase) as an opt-in: "Save account online" copies the on-device profile and workouts.
  Private share links for profile, workouts and chart images; a feed to publish workouts (name, time,
  distance, optional heart rate and route) with follow. Multi-file and folder import; the file picker no
  longer filters by extension so phones can select .fit files.
  Why: sync across devices and share progress, while keeping profiles private by default.

v1 — 2026-10-05
  First version: Garmin FIT/TCX/GPX/ZIP/CSV import, VO₂max (VO2alg1), endurance score, training load,
  trends, records, maps, local profiles.
